before:
This second standalone example follows the failure path.
```javascript
let state = { status: "idle" };
async function load(getData) {
  state = { status: "loading" };
  try {
    const data = await getData();
    state = { status: "success", data };
  } catch (error) {
    state = { status: "error", message: error.message };
  }
}
async function demo() {
  await load(() => Promise.reject(new Error("Try again later")));
  console.log(state.status, state.message);
}
demo();
```
It prints `error Try again later`. The success assignment was skipped when await threw. The catch stored a useful error state rather than pretending data loaded.

